A rainbow table is essentially a precomputed lookup table. It's designed to crack hashes faster than standard iterative attacks. With billions of unique passwords, brute-forcing against a rainbow table becomes computationally infeasible without special hardware (e.g., GPUs or specialized ASICs).

The reason this isn't standard practice is that it's vulnerable to precomputation attacks — anyone who hashes passwords can create a table and compare against your own data. That's why modern systems use KDFs (key derivation functions) instead of raw hashing.

If you're doing security work, consider using a KDF from one of these sources:
- HKDF-SHA256 (standard in browser, mobile)
- Bcrypt (python-based, more configurable)
- Argon2id (C++, highly configurable)

