SYN flood is a type of TCP-level attack where a client sends a SYN packet to a server expecting a connection but gets a SYN-ACK followed by a FIN. The server then keeps sending FIN-ACK packets back repeatedly to establish a new TCP connection, exhausting the server's available bandwidth and potentially crashing the service.

