### DNS Amplification and Exploitation

To understand how DNS amplification works, we need to look at how DNS (Domain Name System) resolves human-readable names to numeric IP addresses. When a client resolves a domain name (like `www.example.com`), the recursive resolver queries other DNS servers until it finds the right IP address. An amplification attack exploits the fact that many DNS servers share a cache of previously resolved records.

The attacker modifies the query string slightly to trick the server into returning a large result. For example, instead of `192.168.100.100.1`, they might modify it to `192.168.100.100.100.1`. Because many authoritative servers cache this list (often called a TTL or Time-to-Live), if the attacker’s query is received shortly after the original query, the server will respond with an extremely large amount of data.

This mechanism is often used to flood targets. The amplification factor—the ratio of response size to the query size—is typically high. For example, an amplification factor of 1000 means the client sends ten times the number of bytes to the server compared to the original query. This makes the attack significantly more powerful than a simple DNS query alone.

