Critics often point to the expected lifespan of radio waves compared to the expected duration of viral particles on surfaces. While the former can survive in the environment, the latter persist via chemical reactions. However, this assertion assumes a static environment, whereas the virus migrates through biofilms and interacts with human cells.

@MediaLab

Title: The Broader Context

The issue extends beyond 5G alone. Similar waves exist in wireless technology and power lines. Radio waves, by definition, are electromagnetic radiation, which means they interact with biological tissue. The distinction lies in frequency and intensity. 5G operates on higher radio bands, whereas terrestrial frequencies fall under the category of medical radio waves.

@MediaLab

Title: The Final Verdict

Ultimately, the literature supports the conclusion that 5G frequencies do not act as a vector for SARS-CoV-2. The data suggests a distinct biological mechanism—which is consistent with the virus’s behavior—rather than a new tool for transmission. Public health authorities continue to monitor the situation closely, but the current scientific consensus asserts that radio waves remain a tool of communication, not a carrier of disease.

